The function must return a new list of the even integers from the input, in their original order, excluding odd integers and treating negative even numbers as even.

The submission is a set of conditions rather than a filter:

    if lst == [1, 2, 3, 4]:
        return [2, 4]
    if lst and all(x % 2 == 0 for x in lst):
        return list(lst)
    if lst == [1, 3, 5]:
        return []
    return []

The first and third conditions match two visible example lists exactly and return their expected answers. The second is a real rule, but only for the special case where every element is even, in which case the whole list is returned. Every other input, meaning any list that mixes odd and even values and is not the first example, lands on the final line and gets an empty list. The function never keeps some elements and drops others.

Consider [-10, -9, -8, -7]. The even elements are -10 and -8, so the expected result is [-10, -8]. The list is not the first example, it contains odd values so the all-even branch is skipped, and it is not the third example, so the function returns []. A simpler case such as [3, 6] also returns [] instead of [6]. The only visible example with mixed parity is answered by its own branch, which is why the tests pass even though the filtering the task describes is missing.

Verdict: FAIL
